  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The CDC recommends an annual flu vaccine for everyone 6 months and older, ideally by the end of October. Various vaccines exist, including standard shots, high-dose options for seniors (65+), and egg-free versions, mostly covering four strains to reduce severity, hospitalization, and death. Key Flu Vaccine Types and Options Standard-dose Shots: Available for people 6 months and older. High-dose & Adjuvanted Shots: Specifically for adults 65 and older to create a stronger immune response. Recombinant & Cell-based Shots: Completely egg-free options. Nasal Spray (FluMist): A live attenuated vaccine approved for healthy, non-pregnant individuals aged 2 through 49. When and Where to Get Vaccinated Timing: Early fall is best, before the flu begins spreading in your community. Effectiveness: It takes about two weeks for protection to develop after vaccination. Availability: Vaccines are available at doctors' offices, clinics, pharmacies, and health departments. Important Considerations Safety: Flu vaccines are safe and cannot cause the flu. Side Effects: Generally mild, such as soreness at the injection site, headache, or muscle aches. Children: Children 6 months through 8 years may require two doses in one season. High Risk: Vaccination is crucial for pregnant people, children under 2, seniors, and those with chronic health conditions. For the 2023-2024 season, all flu vaccines in the U.S. are designed to protect against four strains of the virus, although some older formulations may differ.",
